Description: Quit cleanly when the desktop session ends, as the Inhibit portal reports it.
 A desktop session tells its applications through the session monitor of the Inhibit portal that it ends, while it
 still runs. GTK 4 applications and Chromium quit then. Firefox did not create such a monitor, so it ran until systemd
 stopped it with SIGTERM, at the same time as the compositor, and GTK ends a process whose Wayland connection breaks.
 .
 nsNativeAppSupportUnix is split into a base class and two backends, chosen when Firefox starts:
 nsNativeAppSupportXSMP follows the session through XSMP when SESSION_MANAGER names a session manager, as on KDE
 Plasma, and nsNativeAppSupportDBus through the Inhibit portal otherwise, as on GNOME, or when
 MOZ_DBUS_SESSION_MANAGEMENT=1. --sm-disable turns both off. The D-Bus backend creates a session monitor when it is
 enabled, answers the query whether the session may end at once, and when the session ends, quits with
 nsIAppStartup::Quit(eForceQuit), as the XSMP "die" callback does. A monitor ends with the portal process, so a new
 one is created whenever another process owns the portal's name, and the calls do not start the portal, which stops
 at the end of the session. AsyncDBus.h gains GetPortalSessionPath(), which names the session of the monitor.
Author: Mitar
Bug: https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=2090663
Forwarded: https://phabricator.services.mozilla.com/D340640
